---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import TldrBox from '../../components/TldrBox.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { siteFromHost } from '../../content';

const site = siteFromHost();
const path = '/compare/activecampaign-vs-klaviyo';
const title = 'ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo for Shopify Email: 2026 Comparison';
const desc = 'ActiveCampaign or Klaviyo for a Shopify store? Compare what each tool does well, where it falls short, and what each plan costs at 500 to 25,000 contacts.';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'Which is better for a Shopify store that sells mostly direct to consumer?',
    answer: 'Klaviyo is the better first pick for most direct-to-consumer Shopify stores. Its email plans are priced by active profiles, starting at $20 a month for 500 profiles, and its Shopify setup is built around customer and order events. ActiveCampaign makes more sense when sales pipelines, lead scoring or wholesale follow-up have to run next to the store.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do the two tools compare on price at 1,000 contacts?',
    answer: 'Klaviyo lists $30 a month for an email plan with 1,000 profiles. ActiveCampaign lists a Starter starting price of $15 a month at 1,000 contacts, and its Plus plan starts at $49 a month at the same size. The plans include different features, so the lower ActiveCampaign number does not mean the two offers are equivalent. Match the feature set to your workflows before comparing sticker prices.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Does Klaviyo have a free plan, and does ActiveCampaign?',
    answer: 'Klaviyo has a free plan that covers 250 active profiles, 500 email sends a month and $5 of mobile messages. ActiveCampaign does not list an ongoing free plan in this review. Its pricing page shows a free trial, but this review does not state how long that trial runs.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Which tool is better for wholesale or B2B follow-up on a Shopify store?',
    answer: 'ActiveCampaign is usually the better fit when wholesale inquiries need deal stages, sales follow-up and lead scoring. Klaviyo can segment and branch wholesale contacts, but it is not designed as a sales pipeline. Many stores that sell both direct and wholesale keep the wholesale process in a CRM and use Klaviyo for consumer email and SMS.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Can I move from ActiveCampaign to Klaviyo, or the other way round?',
    answer: 'Yes, but plan the move as a rebuild, not an export. Export contacts with their consent status and custom fields, rebuild the welcome and cart flows first, and keep both platforms from emailing the same person during the overlap. CRM-specific automations such as deal stages usually need a different design in Klaviyo, often built from tags and segments.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Is SMS included in Klaviyo\'s email plan?',
    answer: 'No. The email plans listed here cover email profiles. Klaviyo lists separate Email+SMS plans by profile count, but this review does not show their prices. Read the Email+SMS section of Klaviyo\'s pricing page before you budget for text messages.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />

  <section class="py-14 md:py-20">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="mb-8 text-sm">
        <a class="link" href="/compare">Comparisons</a>
        <span class="mx-2 text-[#5d3a1d]">/</span>
        <span class="text-[#5d3a1d]">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="max-w-4xl">
        <p class="editorial-kicker">Head-to-head</p>
        <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo for Shopify email</h1>
        <p class="mt-7 max-w-3xl text-xl leading-9 text-[#4a2d17]">
          For most Shopify stores that sell directly to consumers, Klaviyo is the better first pick. Its email plan is priced by active profiles, starting at $20 a month for 500 profiles and $30 a month for 1,000, and its Shopify setup is built around customer and order activity. ActiveCampaign is the stronger choice when sales pipelines, lead scoring or wholesale follow-up need to run next to the store, and its Starter plan starts at $15 a month for 1,000 contacts.
        </p>
      </header>

      <div class="mt-12 max-w-4xl">
        <TldrBox
          picks={[
            { label: 'Direct-to-consumer Shopify store', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Shopify-first setup and a per-profile email plan from $20 a month at 500 profiles.' },
            { label: 'Sales pipeline next to the store', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Deal stages and lead scoring sit beside Shopify marketing automation.' },
            { label: 'Budget start below 1,000 contacts', tool: 'Klaviyo', reason: 'Free plan for up to 250 active profiles and 500 sends a month.' },
            { label: 'Wholesale plus retail', tool: 'ActiveCampaign', reason: 'Wholesale inquiries can be handled as a sales process rather than a segment.' },
          ]}
        />
      </div>

      <div class="mt-12 card overflow-x-auto">
        <h2 class="px-5 py-4 font-serif text-2xl font-black text-[#24170f]">Comparison at a glance</h2>
        <table class="data-table">
          <thead>
            <tr><th>Tool</th><th>Best for</th><th>Starting price</th><th>Plan and billing</th><th>Key limitation</th></tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Klaviyo</td>
              <td>Direct-to-consumer Shopify stores that run retention email and SMS</td>
              <td>$20 a month</td>
              <td>Email plan for 500 active profiles, billed monthly in this review; free plan for 250 profiles</td>
              <td>Email+SMS plans are not priced in this review, and a store with a large sales pipeline still needs a CRM</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>ActiveCampaign</td>
              <td>Shopify stores that also run sales pipelines, lead scoring or content nurture</td>
              <td>$15 a month</td>
              <td>Starter plan at 1,000 contacts, billed monthly in this review; no ongoing free plan listed</td>
              <td>Prices above 1,000 contacts are quoted on request, and browse-driven ecommerce flows take more setup</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <article class="prose-shopify mt-14 max-w-4xl">
        <!-- screenshot-unavailable: this review uses the published pricing tables and written feature descriptions instead of vendor interface images -->

        <h2>Who this comparison is for</h2>
        <p>
          Both tools can send email and automated flows to people who buy from a Shopify store, so the question is rarely whether they can do the job at all. The real choice depends on what sits around the store. If the business is mostly consumer orders, with abandoned carts, post-purchase flows and win-back campaigns doing most of the work, Klaviyo is built for that shape. If the business also runs a sales process, with inquiries that move through stages, a sales team that follows up, and content that feeds leads into a funnel, ActiveCampaign gives that process more room.
        </p>
        <p>
          This page is written for Shopify store owners and marketing leads who are choosing between the two, or who are already on one and wondering whether the other would fit better. It does not treat either tool as a universal winner. Prices below come from each vendor's pricing page, as checked on 7 October 2026. Plans and prices change, so confirm them on the vendor page before you sign a contract.
        </p>

        <h2>Klaviyo for Shopify stores</h2>
        <h3>What Klaviyo does well for this audience</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Its Shopify connection is the core of the product. Customer, order and catalog data feed segments and flows without much custom work, which suits stores that want retention email running quickly.</li>
          <li>Flows for abandoned carts, post-purchase follow-ups and win-back campaigns are part of the standard setup, so a small team can launch the core retention program in the first week rather than building each sequence from scratch.</li>
          <li>Segments are shared across email and SMS when you buy both. A customer who opens an email and then receives a text can be measured in one segment model, which makes it easier to see which channel is driving repeat purchases.</li>
          <li>The pricing model is easy to predict. The email plan scales by active profiles, and each step in the price table is a named profile count, so a store can see the next cost jump before it reaches the limit.</li>
          <li>A free plan exists for very small lists. It covers 250 active profiles and 500 email sends a month, which is enough to test a welcome flow and one cart flow before paying.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Where Klaviyo falls short</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Klaviyo is not a sales pipeline. Wholesale or B2B inquiries can be tagged and segmented, but the team that follows up with distributors will not get the deal-stage view that a CRM provides.</li>
          <li>Costs grow with the profile count rather than with the number of messages you send. A store with a large list of inactive people pays for those profiles unless it removes or suppresses them, so list hygiene needs an owner.</li>
          <li>Text messaging is priced separately through Email+SMS plans. This review does not list those prices, so a store that plans to use SMS should get a quote from Klaviyo before comparing totals.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Klaviyo price</h3>
        <p>
          Klaviyo's email plan is billed monthly in this review. It starts at $20 a month for 500 active profiles, $30 a month for 1,000, $60 a month for 2,500, $100 a month for 5,000, $150 a month for 10,000 and $400 a month for 25,000. The free plan covers 250 active profiles, 500 email sends a month and $5 of mobile messages. Annual billing is not listed in this review. The full price table is on <a class="link" href="https://www.klaviyo.com/pricing">Klaviyo's pricing page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>ActiveCampaign for Shopify stores</h2>
        <h3>What ActiveCampaign does well for this audience</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>It combines email marketing, marketing automation and a standard CRM in one product. The Starter plan includes those core pieces with standard CRM and ecommerce integrations, so a store that wants to keep customer records and sales notes together can do it in one tool.</li>
          <li>Deal stages, lead scoring and sales follow-up fit wholesale and distributor inquiries. A team that handles B2B leads can see where each deal sits and which automation fired without leaving the platform.</li>
          <li>Segmentation grows with the plan. Standard segmentation and landing pages appear from the Plus tier, advanced segmentation from Pro, and premium segmentation with a dedicated account team from Enterprise.</li>
          <li>It handles content and sales journeys in the same place. A reader who downloads a guide on a blog, a visitor who requests a sample box and a customer who buys from the Shopify store can sit in one contact record with one history.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Where ActiveCampaign falls short</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Shopify is one of several connectors rather than the center of the product. Browse-driven flows and product-level retention logic usually need more setup than they do in Klaviyo, and some teams end up with custom triggers.</li>
          <li>The published starting prices are for 1,000 contacts. Above that, the price depends on the contact count, and this review does not list those figures, so the cost at 2,500 or 10,000 contacts has to come from ActiveCampaign directly.</li>
          <li>The CRM depth is also a learning curve. A small store that only wants cart recovery may find the pipeline features and automation builder more than it needs during the first months.</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>ActiveCampaign price</h3>
        <p>
          ActiveCampaign's plans are priced by contact count, and the published starting prices are for 1,000 contacts. The Starter plan starts at $15 a month, Plus at $49 a month, Pro at $79 a month and Enterprise at $145 a month. The same monthly figure appears for annual billing in this review. The pricing page lists a free trial, but this review does not state its length. There is no ongoing free plan listed. The full pricing table is on <a class="link" href="https://www.activecampaign.com/pricing">ActiveCampaign's pricing page</a>.
        </p>

        <h2>What each plan costs at real list sizes</h2>
        <p>
          The table below shows the monthly price each vendor lists for the list sizes where a number is published. Where ActiveCampaign does not list a price for a size, the cell says so rather than estimating one.
        </p>
        <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Contacts or active profiles</th><th>Klaviyo email plan, per month</th><th>ActiveCampaign, per month</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>500</td><td>$20 (500-profile email plan)</td><td>Pricing on request</td></tr>
              <tr><td>1,000</td><td>$30 (1,000-profile email plan)</td><td>From $15 (Starter starting price at 1,000 contacts)</td></tr>
              <tr><td>2,500</td><td>$60 (2,500-profile email plan)</td><td>Pricing on request</td></tr>
              <tr><td>5,000</td><td>$100 (5,000-profile email plan)</td><td>Pricing on request</td></tr>
              <tr><td>10,000</td><td>$150 (10,000-profile email plan)</td><td>Pricing on request</td></tr>
              <tr><td>25,000</td><td>$400 (25,000-profile email plan)</td><td>Pricing on request</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          Read the table carefully. The Klaviyo figures are for the email plan only, and Email+SMS prices are not shown here. The ActiveCampaign figure is a starting price for the Starter tier at 1,000 contacts, and it does not describe the cost of a larger list. Because the two vendors price different things in different ways, a direct comparison at 2,500 or 10,000 contacts needs an ActiveCampaign quote.
        </p>
        <p>
          The arithmetic for an annual budget is simple once the monthly figure is known. A store on Klaviyo's 2,500-profile email plan pays $60 a month, so a year on that plan is 12 × $60 = $720 before any SMS plan or add-on. A store on the 10,000-profile plan pays $150 a month, which is 12 × $150 = $1,800 a year. Those totals show the cost of the email plan alone, so an SMS plan has to be added separately.
        </p>

        <h2>ActiveCampaign tiers at 1,000 contacts</h2>
        <p>
          The table below lists ActiveCampaign's four plans at the 1,000-contact starting point. It shows the feature groups each plan includes, so a store can match its workflows to the lowest plan that covers them.
        </p>
        <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>Plan</th><th>Starting price at 1,000 contacts</th><th>What the plan adds</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Starter</td><td>$15 a month</td><td>Email marketing, marketing automation, standard CRM and ecommerce integrations</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Plus</td><td>$49 a month</td><td>Standard segmentation and landing pages</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Pro</td><td>$79 a month</td><td>Advanced segmentation</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Enterprise</td><td>$145 a month</td><td>Premium segmentation and a dedicated account team</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
        <p>
          A Shopify store that wants advanced segmentation at 1,000 contacts has to move past Starter. At that size, the difference between Starter at $15 and Pro at $79 is $64 a month, or 12 × $64 = $768 a year. Whether that is worth paying depends on how much the segmentation changes the sends. A store that only needs cart and welcome flows may not need Pro at all.
        </p>

        <h2>Decision table: which tool fits which need</h2>
        <p>
          The table below turns the comparison into a set of decisions. Each row names a need, the tool that fits it better and the reason. Use it as a starting point, then check the reason against your own order volume and team structure.
        </p>
        <div class="card overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="data-table">
            <thead>
              <tr><th>If you need</th><th>Pick</th><th>Because</th></tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td>Retention email that reacts to Shopify orders and carts</td><td>Klaviyo</td><td>The Shopify connection and standard flows are the core of the product</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Deal stages for wholesale or distributor leads</td><td>ActiveCampaign</td><td>The CRM and sales automation are built for pipeline follow-up</td></tr>
              <tr><td>One segment model across email and SMS</td><td>Klaviyo</td><td>Email and SMS are managed in the same segment structure, though SMS pricing needs a quote</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Lowest cost for a small list with a free start</td><td>Klaviyo</td><td>The free plan covers 250 active profiles and 500 sends a month</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Blog readers and shoppers in one contact history</td><td>ActiveCampaign</td><td>Content engagement and store activity can sit on one record</td></tr>
              <tr><td>Predictable cost at 2,500 to 25,000 profiles</td><td>Klaviyo</td><td>Every listed profile count has a published monthly price</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>

        <h2>How to choose</h2>
        <p>
          Start with the revenue shape, not the feature list. Ask what share of revenue comes from Shopify orders and what share comes from sales that need a follow-up process. If most revenue is consumer orders, the work is retention: welcome series, cart recovery, post-purchase emails and win-back flows. That work favors Klaviyo, because the store data is already the main input. If a meaningful share of revenue comes from wholesale, distributors, memberships or other sales-led deals, the work includes pipeline follow-up, and that favors ActiveCampaign.
        </p>
        <p>
          Next, check the list size and the price at that size. For a store with 500 to 2,500 profiles, Klaviyo's published price is easy to read, and the free plan allows a test before any payment. For a store above 1,000 contacts on ActiveCampaign, request a quote, because the starting price does not describe the cost of a larger list. Put both totals into the same twelve-month table before deciding.
        </p>
        <p>
          Finally, decide who will run the tool. Klaviyo asks for someone who can maintain segments and suppression lists, and it rewards that work with clear reporting. ActiveCampaign asks for someone who can design and maintain pipelines and tags, and it rewards that work with a CRM that the sales team can use. A store that has neither role in place should choose the tool whose setup it can finish in the first month.
        </p>

        <h2>Setup and migration checklist</h2>
        <p>
          Whether you are starting fresh or moving from one tool to the other, the same steps apply. A migration is a rebuild of the workflows, so plan it as a project with an owner and a date.
        </p>
        <ol>
          <li>Export the contact list with consent status, subscription dates and the custom fields that drive segments. Remove addresses that have bounced, unsubscribed or never opened in the last six months before you import anything.</li>
          <li>Map each automation in the current tool to a named equivalent in the new one. Deal stages in ActiveCampaign usually become tags and segments in Klaviyo, and browse flows in Klaviyo need a custom build in ActiveCampaign.</li>
          <li>Rebuild the welcome series and the cart flow first, because those carry the most revenue for most stores and are the easiest to test.</li>
          <li>Run both tools in parallel for a short overlap, and use suppression so that the same person does not receive two sequences for the same event.</li>
          <li>Check that the Shopify connection sends order and cart events into the new tool, and place a test order to confirm that the right flow fires.</li>
          <li>Set a retirement date for the old flows, and archive the old tool's contact data in a form you can still read.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>A five-flow test before you commit</h2>
        <p>
          A test is more useful than a feature list. Choose five flows that the store runs every week and build each one in both trials, if you can. Use the same products and the same consent rules so the results are comparable.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Welcome flow, with branches for the signup source, such as a pop-up, a footer form or a content download.</li>
          <li>Cart abandonment, with a rule that skips people who have already bought.</li>
          <li>Post-purchase flow, with an exclusion for gift buyers and an optional review request.</li>
          <li>Win-back flow, with separate paths for engaged and unengaged contacts.</li>
          <li>One follow-up tied to a CRM event, such as a wholesale inquiry, if the business has one. If it does not, skip this flow and say so in your notes.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Score each flow on how long it takes to set up, how reliably the Shopify event fires, how easy it is to see why a person was included, and how much manual tagging it needs. Where the results differ, the flow that a non-technical team member can change without help is usually the better long-term fit.
        </p>

        <h2>Common mistakes when choosing</h2>
        <p>
          The most common mistake is buying a plan for the list you hope to have, rather than the one you have now. Both tools bill for contacts or profiles, so a large, stale list raises the cost of every send. Clean the list first, then request the quote.
        </p>
        <p>
          The second mistake is comparing a starting price with a full price. ActiveCampaign's $15 figure is a Starter price at 1,000 contacts, and the Plus, Pro and Enterprise plans at the same size cost more. Klaviyo's $20 figure is an email plan at 500 profiles, and SMS is priced separately. Compare the plan you would actually run, at the list size you actually have.
        </p>
        <p>
          The third mistake is choosing on the agency's preference. A partner who knows one platform well may recommend it for every client. Ask for a written explanation of how the recommended tool handles your order events, your wholesale process and your list size, and compare that answer with the two-column table above.
        </p>

        <h2>Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          For a Shopify store that sells mainly to consumers, Klaviyo is the better first pick. Its Shopify setup, its standard retention flows and its clear per-profile pricing suit that shape of business, and its free plan lets a small store test before paying. ActiveCampaign is the better pick when sales pipelines, lead scoring and wholesale follow-up sit alongside the store, because its CRM and sales automation are built for that work. Stores that run both should decide which process needs the most control and build the other around it.
        </p>
      </article>

      <div class="mt-10 max-w-4xl text-sm">
        <p class="font-black text-[#24170f]">Related comparisons</p>
        <p class="mt-2">
          <a class="link" href="/compare/drip-vs-activecampaign">Drip vs ActiveCampaign</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-mailchimp">Klaviyo vs Mailchimp</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/compare/klaviyo-vs-drip">Klaviyo vs Drip</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/for/supplements">Email apps for supplement stores</a> ·
          <a class="link" href="/use-cases/abandoned-cart">Abandoned cart playbook</a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="ActiveCampaign vs Klaviyo FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
